Encoding in Beluga

Here we demonstrate the particularly elegant encoding of this proof
in Beluga using first class (simultaneous) substitutions. By hand
we defined grammar and typing separately, but here it is actually
more convenient to define intrinsically typed terms directly. Below,
tm defines our family of simply-typed lambda terms indexed by
their type as an LF signature. In typical higher-order abstract syntax fashion, lambda abstraction takes a function representing the
abstraction of a term over a variable. There is no case for variables,
as they are treated implicitly. We remind the reader that this is a
weak, representational function space – there is no case analysis
or recursion, and hence only genuine lambda terms can be represented.

